      "A fixed 14% slope gives a hard uphill walk from the first step",
      "Folds flat and is light enough to move on its wheels",
      "The same fixed incline appears on Sunny’s page and in independent coverage",
    ],
    cons: [
      "You will need the handrails; a belt you push yourself at a steep slope is not for running",
      "Sunny now lists 330 lb; the Amazon title this site read in October said 300 lb, so no capacity is printed",
      "Only one slope; you cannot lower it for an easy day",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The cheapest way into Sunny’s range and a reasonable buy for steep walking in a small room, as long as you accept a manual belt and a single slope.",
    description: "The SF-T1407SMART is Sunny’s manual walking treadmill, and the one Sunny model this site has recommended before, on our best manual treadmills list. There is no motor: you drive the belt with your feet, against two flywheels, on a deck fixed at a 14% slope, and a small console tracks time, speed, distance and calories and connects to the SunnyFit app. Sunny listed it at $199.99 on 5 November 2026. The capacity is the figure to be careful with: Sunny’s page now gives 330 lb, the Amazon title this site read in October gave 300 lb, and the listing’s own bullet points said 330 lb, so plan on 300 lb until the box in front of you says otherwise. The older SF-T1407M without Bluetooth still appears at some retailers with a 220 lb rating and different figures, so check the model number. Our guide to how to use a manual treadmill covers the technique a slope this steep needs.",
  },
